  "summary": "White running stitches quilting indigo layers into asanoha stars and seigaiha waves — warmth made visible.",
  "description": "Sashiko (little stabs) began as necessity: Edo-era northern farmers, barred from cotton, layered and stitched recycled indigo cloth for warmth, using even white running stitches that doubled as reinforcement and quiet ornament — asanoha hemp stars, seigaiha wave scales, tortoise-shell kikkō grids. Firefighter hanten jackets wore dense sashiko quilting; fisher jackets got tally stitches for every catch. Boro patchwork grew from the same economy, and today sashiko leads the global visible-mending movement.",
